Sections: How it works · Quick start · Directives · Typing into several elements ·
Erasing · Cursor · Configuration · Controlling a run · Errors · Recipes · Notes and limits

REASON
AutoWriter was the only module in the repository with neither a guide nor an example;
doc/README.md listed its guide as pending. Written to the pattern of the existing guides
(mechanism first, then quick start, then a configuration table, then the parts that
surprise people).

Three things are documented that a reader could not infer from the other guides, because
AutoWriter departs from the house pattern:

  - There is NO initialize(). Every other guide's lifecycle is construct → configure →
    initialize; this component is driven by method calls and has nothing to activate.
    Stated at the top, where a reader following doc/README.md's four-step lifecycle will
    hit it before trying to call a method that does not exist.
  - The component owns the inside of its target, appending and clearing a
    '.vAutoWriter' span, so the target must be an empty container.
  - The caret is steady while typing, blinks ONLY during a '~n~' pause, and hides when
    the run ends. That is not the usual typewriter convention, so the guide states the
    three states plainly and gives the trick for leaving a caret blinking on screen
    (end the string with a long pause).

Also documented: the '*n*' / '~n~' / '|' directive syntax and its lack of an escape
sequence; that directives are validated when writeText() is called rather than when they
are reached; that a "unit" is a character or a line break, which is what makes erasing
back through multi-line text behave; that collections are copied at the start of a run;
that deleteText() resolves to the written span; that config is write-only and cannot be
read back; and that stop() is the only way to cancel a run, needed on teardown because a
run whose target has left the document keeps typing into a detached node.
